+Snapshot validation, index preparation, activation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite database. Each kiosk builds its own index from a validated snapshot and serves every lookup from that local database; no lookup path uses the network or a service. The certificate interface and behavior are unchanged by this choice.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Uniqueness is checked after applying the same normalization used at lookup. Build the replacement index in a staging location while the active index continues serving lookups. Records are written only to staging and are never visible to lookups before activation. Parse the snapshot as a stream so a 150 MB snapshot is not held in memory at once. A failed import discards staging, leaves the previous snapshot active, and reports a useful error identifying the failed check.
+
+## Index Build and Activation
+
+Each snapshot produces an SQLite database whose records table is keyed by normalized serial number and whose metadata holds the snapshot date and index schema version. Build the database as a complete file in staging, close it, and make it durable before activation.
+
+Activation atomically replaces a small active-index pointer file that names the current database. The previous database remains in place until the new pointer is durable, then is removed. A crash during import therefore leaves either the previous complete snapshot or the new complete snapshot active, never a partially built database. Lookups open the active database read-only.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the records table by exact normalized serial number, which is a primary-key lookup over approximately 90,000 rows. Return the matching record with the active snapshot date, or return NotFound with the active snapshot date when no row matches. A record read is answered from the local database without network access.
+
+## Failure Handling
+
+A snapshot with an invalid signature, an unsupported schema version, duplicate normalized serial numbers, or a malformed record is rejected before activation; the active snapshot remains available. A build failure, including running out of disk space, discards staging and leaves the active snapshot in place. When the active index is missing or unreadable, the registry reports an operational failure rather than returning NotFound, so absence of a record is never inferred from a broken index.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index built from each validated snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Only this option satisfies the HLD tenet that all lookups complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access. It keeps the registry self-contained and accepts the per-kiosk rebuild cost on each snapshot import. The directory option would add a lab-network dependency and timeout handling; the managed service would add recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity. |
+| Snapshot activation | Build a versioned database in staging, then atomically replace an active-index pointer | Overwrite the active database in place; rebuild lazily on first lookup | The pointer switch keeps every lookup on a complete snapshot and lets a failed or interrupted import leave the previous snapshot active. In-place overwrite or lazy rebuild would expose partial or absent data. |
+| Serial uniqueness | Reject snapshots whose serial numbers collide after lookup normalization | Reject only byte-identical duplicate serial numbers | Lookup normalization would otherwise make two records reachable by the same request, making the result dependent on storage order. Rejecting at import keeps lookup deterministic. |
+| Index access | Open the active database read-only | Open read-write | Registry records are read-only on the kiosk, so lookup code should be unable to mutate the active snapshot. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Lookup backend: local embedded SQLite index, recorded in Decisions & Alternatives above.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Index schema migration on application upgrade: whether to retain the source snapshot on the kiosk for rebuilding or require the snapshot to be imported again.
+2. Presentation of an unreadable or missing active index to the technician; the registry surfaces an operational failure, and how the certificates node presents that failure is a downstream cascade.
+3. Disk-space budget for staging a new database alongside the previous database on each kiosk.
